1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Shard keys: distribution and targeting. Which records land together, and which shards must a query visit?

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,500
The application talks to mongos, a router. It never needs to know where a record lives.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Config servers hold the cluster metadata, including which shard owns which key range.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:15,500
Each shard stores part of the collection and is normally its own replica set.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,200
Atlas Free cannot create this. We analyze candidate keys instead of measuring a cluster.

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:24,800
Cardinality counts distinct values. Frequency asks how often the most common one repeats.

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:27,800
These describe stored records, not traffic: one popular ID could still get most requests.

8
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:37,800
Sort by dateAdded. The oldest 60 records set three boundaries: 15 per range.

9
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:40,800
All 15 land in the open-ended last range. Monotonic keys make a write hotspot there.

10
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:50,300
Ranges still serve date-window queries well. Real MongoDB also splits and rebalances ranges.

11
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,000
The same 15 records: 3, 3, 6, 3. Check: they add up to 15.

12
00:00:53,500 --> 00:01:00,400
Distinct IDs give the hash something to spread. Neighboring dates no longer sit together.

13
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:02,900
Key on vendor instead: all 16 Microsoft records hash to the same bucket.

14
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,400
Same input, same hash. Hashing can't split a repeated value.

15
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:15,366
The filter carries the shard-key value, so mongos can target one shard.

16
00:01:15,866 --> 00:01:18,066
No shard key in the filter: the query may go to every shard (scatter-gather).

17
00:01:18,566 --> 00:01:21,366
A vendor index helps inside each shard, but it doesn't tell the router which shards to skip.

18
00:01:21,866 --> 00:01:24,666
Counting all records by vendor needs every shard anyway: that's the question's cost, not a mistake.

19
00:01:27,266 --> 00:01:37,266
Four shard-key questions: Cardinality: how many distinct values? Frequency: does one value dominate? Hashing can't split it. Monotonicity: do new values pile into the last range? Targeting: do common queries include the key? Measure the real queries; no single property guarantees all four.

